{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>And ten women shall bake.</b> This number is used for multitude in many places in the Torah, and likewise (Job 19) ״זה עשר פעמים תכלימוני״, and because it is the end of all counting and everything is included in it, therefore this number is taken to indicate multitude. And so we find the number seven is also used for multitude (Proverbs 24): ״כי שבע יפול צדיק וקם״, and it is written of Jacob (Genesis 33): ״וישתחו ארצה שבע פעמים״, and it is written (I Samuel 2): ״עד עקרה ילדה שבעה״ — that is, a multitude of children. And the reason that seven is used for multitude is that you will not find in the entire alphabet a pair of letters in sequence whose numerical value is seven except ג״ד, and its reverse ד״ג, which is a species of multitude, in the manner of what is written (Genesis 48): ״וידגו לרוב״, and therefore the number seven is taken for multitude like the number ten."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:28:48.038288038Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}